```java
class Builder {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    // Use 1: Disambiguation — field vs parameter
    Builder(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;  // this.name = field, name = parameter
        this.age = age;
    }

    // Use 2: Constructor chaining — call another constructor
    Builder() {
        this("Unknown", 0); // calls 2-arg constructor
    }

    // Use 3: Pass current object as argument
    void register(Registry r) {
        r.add(this); // passing this object to another method
    }

    // Use 4: Return current object (fluent/builder pattern)
    Builder setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        return this; // enables method chaining: b.setName("X").setAge(5)
    }
}
```

`this` cannot be used in `static` methods — static methods have no associated object, so `this` is undefined.

```java
class Animal {
    String sound = "...";
    void move() { System.out.println("Animal moving"); }
    Animal(String name) { System.out.println("Animal: " + name); }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    String sound = "Woof"; // hides parent field

    Dog(String name) {
        super(name);           // Use 1: call parent constructor (FIRST statement)
    }

    @Override
    void move() {
        super.move();          // Use 2: call parent method (extend, not replace)
        System.out.println("Dog running");
    }

    void showSounds() {
        System.out.println(super.sound); // Use 3: access parent field
        System.out.println(this.sound);  // own field
    }
}
```

`super` is not a reference you can store or pass around. You cannot do `Animal parent = super`. It's a syntactic construct to reach the parent class. Also, `super` cannot be used in static methods.

```java
class Animal {
    Animal(String name) { System.out.println("Animal: " + name); }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        this("DefaultDog"); // OK — calls Dog(String)
        // super("Animal"); // COMPILE ERROR — can't have both!
    }

    Dog(String name) {
        super(name); // THIS constructor calls super — the chain reaches parent
        System.out.println("Dog: " + name);
    }
}
```

When `Dog()` calls `this("DefaultDog")`, it delegates to `Dog(String name)`, which calls `super(name)`. The parent constructor still gets called — just through the delegation chain.

The rule makes logical sense: `super()` initializes the parent object, `this()` redirects to another constructor. You can't do both from one constructor — you'd be saying "initialize parent first" AND "redirect to sibling first" simultaneously. The solution is to let the delegated constructor handle the `super()` call.

```java
class Parent {
    void setup() { System.out.println("Parent setup"); }

    void init() {
        this.setup(); // 'this' dispatches dynamically!
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void setup() { System.out.println("Child setup"); }
}

Child c = new Child();
c.init();
// Output: Child setup  ← NOT 'Parent setup'
```

`c.init()` calls `Parent.init()`, which calls `this.setup()`. Since `this` is the `Child` object, and `Child` overrides `setup()`, the Child's version runs.

**The dangerous gotcha — calling overridable methods from constructors:**
```java
class Parent {
    Parent() { this.setup(); } // DANGER: setup() may be overridden!
    void setup() { System.out.println("Parent"); }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    private int value = 42;
    @Override void setup() { System.out.println("value: " + value); }
}
new Child(); // prints: value: 0  — value not initialized yet!
```
When `Parent()` runs, `Child`'s `value` field isn't initialized yet (default 0).

```java
class Counter {
    private int count;
    private static int total;

    // Instance method — has 'this'
    void increment() {
        this.count++;  // OK — 'this' is the current Counter object
    }

    // Static method — NO 'this'
    static void resetTotal() {
        total = 0;       // OK — static field, no object needed
        // this.count = 0; // COMPILE ERROR — 'this' not available in static context
        // super.toString(); // COMPILE ERROR — 'super' not available either
    }
}

// Static method can be called WITHOUT an object:
Counter.resetTotal(); // no Counter object exists here — so what would 'this' refer to?
```

The logical reason: `Counter.resetTotal()` runs without any `Counter` object being created. There IS no `this` — there's no object for `this` to point to. The same reasoning applies to `super`.

A **static method** is essentially a class-level function; it operates on the class, not on an instance.

When you override a method, you have two choices:
1. **Replace**: completely ignore parent's implementation (risky in frameworks)
2. **Extend**: call `super.method()` and add to it

```java
class Logger {
    void log(String msg) {
        System.out.println("[LOG] " + msg);
    }
}

class TimestampLogger extends Logger {
    @Override
    void log(String msg) {
        // Extend: call parent first, then add behavior
        super.log(msg);  // [LOG] message
        System.out.println("  Logged at: " + java.time.LocalTime.now());
    }
}
```

**Real-world importance**: Android's `onCreate()` requires `super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)` — failing to call it throws an exception. Spring Security's filter chains often need `super.doFilter()`. Without `super.method()`, you lose the crucial parent logic.

Rule of thumb: when in doubt whether to call `super.method()`, check the framework documentation — it's often mandatory.
